A||||8||dennis_6||Fuel Pump Relay||||9||
Z||000000||dennis_6||10-25-2001||04:08 PM||||I took the fuel pump relays off to test them and haynes said that the pins were clearly marked.  This is not so.  The only writing on the relays (I had to take both off since they were connected) were the part numbers.<BR>The part numbers are:10040035 and 5217.  <P>Both of the numbers are found on both relays.<BR>The connectors are in a shape of an L.  Does anyone have the pinout so I can test the relays?  <P>Also I found a metal tube right next to the air filter hooked to nothing!!  The car has ran before, so I assume it is nothing critical.  If you know what it is please let me know.  Thanks!||||reg||9
Z||000001||dennis_6||10-25-2001||06:12 PM||||Replaced the relay and no dice.  I can hear the fuel pump run.  Still very weak fuel pressure.  What can cause this?  I still don't know what the unconnected tube is.||||reg||5
Z||000002||StuGood||10-25-2001||06:41 PM||||If yours is a V6, the unconnected tube near the air cleaner, is the result of a recall.  Recall instructions call for installing a replacement breather tube (that comes with the recall package), and leaving the old tube unconnected.<P>It <B>does</B> look weird, that big pipe sitting there going nowhere.  But since it's preassembled (welded) to another tube along the forward firewall, it was easier to just leave it there.  FWIW, on my GT, I took the tube assembly off, carefully cut out the unused tube, and bolted what was left back in its place.  I did it partly because I got tired of people "discovering" the open pipe -  "Hey-!  What's this?  That doesn't look right..."     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Well, you can't blame them, really.  It really <B>doesn't</B> look quite right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||10-25-2001||08:34 PM||||The low fuel pressure may be caused by a dirty fuel filter-under the car at the right hand forward corner of the engine bay.  I did have a problem once with low fuel pressure that was caused by a split in the rubber tube that couples the pump to the fuel line inside the tank.  I was convinced that the pump had died and dropped the tank and removed it.  Just happened to notice the split in the fat rubber hose that connects the pump to the fuel line before I went out to buy a new pump!<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000004||DR650SE||10-25-2001||08:56 PM||||I just had a very similiar problem turns out im only the biggest idiot and very incompetent.  Check your fuse under the dash.  I had spent endless hours tracing wires and such.  Also the A_B_C_D on the relay is marked where the relay plugs in.  IT shows their on the side.  Hope this helps.  Also if the relay is the problem, switch it with the A/C relay.  That is if you have the A/C.  The A/C relay is right next to the fuel pump relay.  E-mail me with your results please if you don't mind.  DR650SE@aol.com||||reg||1
Z||000005||dennis_6||10-25-2001||09:03 PM||||Thanks guys,<BR>The relay was good.<BR>I doubt the fuel filter is that dirty.<BR>It has to either be the f pump or a split in the connecting line.  Anyway to test just the fuel pump?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||dennis_6||10-25-2001||09:19 PM||||Is there anyway to put a different fuel pump outside the tank?  I never want to drop the tank again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||grinthock||10-25-2001||09:57 PM||||I have gone through some fuel pump things before.<P>Had the fuel pump relay die once, the socket corroded, so I just went to the hardware store and bought a good 25 amp 12V relay and replaced it, no problems since AND it's a standard relay easily replaceable.<P><BR>ALSO, the ECM controls the Fuel Pump as well, when you turn the key the fuel pump RELAY is turned on by the ECM!  There is a relay in the ECM as well (a small small small one, bottom right hand side, looks like a regulator), I had that go in my ECM.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000008||dennis_6||10-25-2001||10:09 PM||||Thanks Grinthock.||||reg||1
